    {
      "id": 17592186050845,
      "mathAcademyId": 1424,
      "title": "The Zero Product Rule for Solving Quadratic Equations",
      "uuid": "6b2f9405-d642-57d9-b6da-adccbbb39242"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186065344,
      "id": "10dfc92a-69a2-5242-9398-777591e6118e",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+5x+6=0\n$$\n\nThe square root method won't work here, since there is a linear term. We can't factor out the variable, either, since there is a constant term. So, what should we do?\n\nOne way to solve this is to factor the entire trinomial on the left-hand side. To do this, we need to find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to give $6$ (the constant term) and\n- add to give $5$ (the linear coefficient).\n\nThese two numbers are $2$ and $3$. So, we can factor the left-hand-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + \\underbrace{2x + 3x}_{5x} + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (3x + 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx+3=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-3 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3,-2$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "1625",
      "stepId": "17592186065241",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186063981,
      "id": "13475711-12de-5c68-a44b-3af6d1cc5a14",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186064331,
        "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Leading Coefficient is Positive"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $x^2-5x +6=0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1421",
      "stepId": "17592186065242",
      "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Leading Coefficient is Positive",
      "workedSolution": "We will factor the left-hand side and then apply the zero product rule.\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $6$ and add to $-5$. These two numbers are $-2$ and $-3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2 - 5x + 6 &= 0 \\\\[5pt] x^2 \\underbrace{-3x -2x}_{\\large -5x} + 6 &= 0 \\\\[5pt] x(x-3) - 2(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[5pt] (x - 2)(x -3) &= 0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-2=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-3=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=2 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2,3$."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186064007,
      "id": "e14c7ea4-da44-5c9e-b7f2-36ad4259963d",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186064340,
        "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Leading Coefficient is Negative"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $-x^2+7x+18=0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1792",
      "stepId": "17592186065255",
      "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Leading Coefficient is Negative",
      "workedSolution": "First, we multiply by $-1$ so that the leading coefficient becomes positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 7x + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(-1)(-x^{2} + 7x + 18) &= (-1) \\cdot (0) \\\\\nx^{2} - 7x - 18 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the equation, we need two numbers that multiply to $-18$ and add to $-7$. These two numbers are $2$ and $-9$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x - 18 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + (2x - 9x) - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-9x - 18) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 9(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 9)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-9=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=9 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-2,9$."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186063982,
      "id": "1fc8c98b-b11c-54cb-bb34-64cd690f4507",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186064332,
        "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Right-Hand Side is Not Zero"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve $-x^2-5x-14=5x-5$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1423",
      "stepId": "17592186065243",
      "title": "Solving by Factoring When the Right-Hand Side is Not Zero",
      "workedSolution": "First, we bring all terms to the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} - 5x - 14 &= 5x - 5 \\\\\n- x^{2} - 5x - 14 - 5x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n- x^{2} + (-5x - 5x) + (-14 + 5) &= 0 \\\\\n- x^{2} - 10x - 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply by $-1$ so that the leading coefficient becomes positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} - 10x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(-1)(-x^{2} - 10x - 9) &= (-1) \\cdot (0) \\\\\nx^{2} + 10x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the equation, we need two numbers that multiply to $9$ and add to $10$. These two numbers are $1$ and $9$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 10x + 9 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + (1x + 9x) + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + x) + (9x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 1) + 9(x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 9)(x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx+9=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-9 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-9,-1$."
    }
